    "narrative": "# Robin's 2025 Outside Memory\n\n## Welcome Message\n\nWelcome back, Robin! As one of our most engaged members across the Outside ecosystem, you've been an absolute powerhouse this year\u2014logging nearly 22,000 interactions across our platforms. Your passion for cycling, adventure, and the outdoors has been a joy to witness, and we're thrilled to celebrate your 2025 journey with us.\n\n---\n\n## Robin's 2025 Outside Memory\n\nYour year started quietly in January, but by February you were already diving deep into the Outside family of publications. What began as casual browsing evolved into something much more purposeful: a year-long love affair with professional cycling, punctuated by genuine adventures in the real world.\n\nThe real turning point came in **June and July**, when your engagement skyrocketed. These were the months of the Tour de France, and you became absolutely consumed by it\u2014tracking every stage, following every key rider, and devouring analysis from cycling's best storytellers. Your interest in **Tadej Poga\u010dar** (178 interactions) and **Jonas Vingegaard** (143 interactions) wasn't casual fandom; it was obsessive in the best way. You were reading Jim Cotton's race coverage, Shane Stokes' tactical breakdowns, and Andrew Hood's insider perspective with the intensity of someone who couldn't miss a single detail.\n\nBut here's what made your year special: you weren't *only* a cycling enthusiast. While VeloNews became your second home (3,843 events), you maintained a rich, curious engagement across Outside Online's broader universe. You read about **Jon Krakauer defending \"Into Thin Air\"** against YouTube critics. You explored pieces on **microplastics in the environment**, **sex differences in sport**, and the science behind **vitamin D supplementation**. You engaged with relationship advice columns and culture pieces. You were, in essence, a complete Outside reader\u2014someone who appreciates both the adrenaline of professional racing and the reflective depth of long-form outdoor journalism.\n\nBy **August and September**, your engagement peaked at its absolute highest (4,574 and 3,790 events respectively). These were the months of the Vuelta a Espa\u00f1a and the transition into fall racing season. Your reading habits during this period reveal someone checking in multiple times daily\u2014often during afternoon work hours (peak activity at 1-2 PM and 6-8 PM), but also burning the midnight oil, with significant engagement at 11 PM and beyond. You weren't just reading; you were *living* the cycling calendar.\n\n---\n\n## Your Passions\n\n**Professional Road Cycling**\nYour engagement with cycling news and analysis is unmatched. With 200 interactions tagged \"Tour de France,\" 178 focused on Tadej Poga\u010dar, and deep dives into riders like Remco Evenepoel, Jonas Vingegaard, and Mathieu van der Poel, you're following the sport at a professional level. You're reading VeloNews religiously and consuming tactical race coverage.\n\n**Competitive Athletes & Training**\nBeyond just watching, you're interested in how the best athletes prepare. You've engaged with content about Visma-Lease a Bike's secret winter training protocols, FasCAT coaching methodologies, and winter training strategies. Articles like \"Why Kristian Blummenfelt Counts Calories\" and pieces on athlete nutrition show you want to understand the science of elite performance.\n\n**Mountain Biking & Trail Culture**\nYour consistent engagement with Trailforks (3,452 combined events across app and web), map exploration, and trail reports reveals a genuine commitment to local trail communities. You're not just reading about trails\u2014you're actively exploring them, saving activities, and participating in the Trailforks community (username: tourmalet1).\n\n**Public Lands & National Parks**\nWith 65 interactions tagged \"public-lands\" and 59 tagged \"national-parks,\" including specific interest in Yosemite (45 interactions), you care deeply about access, conservation, and outdoor adventure. You engaged with pieces on the Pacific Crest Trail border controversy and broader environmental issues.\n\n**Outdoor Gear & Design**\nYour 43 interactions each with \"footwear\" and \"sandals\" tags, plus broader engagement with design-and-tech content (47 interactions), show you're someone who thinks deeply about the equipment that enables adventure. You're interested in both the practical and the innovative.\n\n**Science & Wellness**\nYou've consistently engaged with health and science-focused content\u2014from nutrition and supplementation to environmental health concerns. Your curiosity extends beyond sports performance into genuine wellness questions, showing a holistic approach to outdoor living.\n\n---\n\n## Reading Habits\n\nYour reading pattern reveals someone with a structured life punctuated by cycling obsession. You're most active during **weekday afternoons (12-3 PM)** and **evenings (6-8 PM)**, suggesting you're checking in during work breaks and after-work wind-down sessions. But here's what's fascinating: you have a significant **late-night reading habit**, with your second-highest activity window between **11 PM and 2 AM**. You're the type who gets pulled into a rabbit hole of cycling analysis at midnight and suddenly it's 1 AM. Weekdays dominate your engagement (Monday-Friday account for 18,566 of your 21,568 total events), though you maintain consistent weekend activity\u2014particularly on Sundays (2,252 events), suggesting leisurely weekend browsing.\n\nYour favorite authors tell the story of your cycling obsession: **Jim Cotton** (61 interactions) is your go-to for comprehensive race coverage, **Andrew Hood** (53 interactions) for insider perspective, and **Shane Stokes** (36 interactions) for tactical analysis. You've also engaged meaningfully with **Frederick Dreier**, **Will Tracy**, and **Alex Hutchinson**, showing you appreciate diverse voices in cycling journalism. But your author list also includes **Blair Braverman** (8 interactions), whose \"Tough Love\" relationship column suggests you appreciate Outside's culture and advice content too.\n\nYour engagement spiked dramatically during major cycling events\u2014the Tour de France in July, the Vuelta in September\u2014but you've maintained consistent engagement year-round. You're equally comfortable diving into a 3,000-word feature about evergreen topics as you are refreshing race results. You've read 959 unique articles across 97 different authors, demonstrating genuine breadth alongside your cycling depth. You're not a one-note reader; you're a curious, engaged member of the Outside community who happens to be *really* into professional cycling.",
